  "pd": {
    "basis": "published-pre-1930",
    "detail": "Anonymous American ballad about the 1899 St. Louis shooting of Allen Britt by Frankie Baker; no author is credited on the underlying folk song. The earliest related American printing is Hughie Cannon's 'He Done Me Wrong' of 1904, and the standard sheet is 'Frankie and Johnny' by the Leighton Brothers and Ren Shields, published in the United States in 1912. Both are decades clear of the 1930 line.",
    "sourceEdition": "The traditional twelve-bar ballad tune and ragtime-turnback changes as commonly printed in American folk-song collections, read against the 1912 Leighton Bros. and Ren Shields sheet music; not any later recorded arrangement",
    "notes": "Melody and changes only; no lyrics. Often printed in 2/4 or cut time, which maps to 4/4 at half the written tempo: one encoded bar covers two printed 2/4 bars, so the twelve-bar form is preserved rather than doubled. The common-practice folk version is encoded, not the Sam Cooke or any other later copyrighted arrangement."
  },
